<worksheet>
<title>LT 9 Exponential Graphs</title>
<exercise>
 <introduction>
  <p> Look at the image below.  The graph is of the function <m>f(x)=3(2)^x</m>.  Use the graph to answer the following questions.  You may use Desmos to help you answer the questions.
  </p>

<image width="40%">
      <shortdescription>Graph of f of x equals 3 times 2 to the x on coordinate axes ranging from -4 to 15.</shortdescription>
      
    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-exponential-a"> 
<diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
<definition>f(x)=3*(2)^x</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-4,-4,3,15)">
<grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" hticks="(-4,1,3)" vticks="(-2.5,2.5,15)"/>
<graph function="f"/>
</coordinates>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</introduction>
<task>
  <statement>
    <p> What is the initial value of the function? </p>
  </statement>
  <answer>
    <p> The initial value is <m>3</m>.  This is the value of the function when <m>x=0</m>.  The initial value is also the <m>y</m>-intercept of the graph.  The initial value is also the value of <m>a</m> in the function <m>f(x)=ab^x</m>.</p>
  </answer>
</task>
<task>
  <statement>
    <p> What is the growth factor of the function? </p>
  </statement>
  <answer>
    <p> The growth factor is <m>2</m>.  This is the value of <m>b</m> in the function <m>f(x)=ab^x</m>.  The growth factor is also the value that the function increases by when <m>x</m> increases by 1.  The growth factor is also the value that the function decreases by when <m>x</m> increases by 1 if the function is a decay function.</p>
  </answer>
</task>
<task>
  <statement>
    <p> What is the growth rate of the function? </p>
  </statement>
  <answer>
    <p> The growth rate is <m>1</m> or <m>100%</m>.  The growth rate is the growth factor minus 1.  The growth rate is also the growth factor minus 1 expressed as a percent.  The growth rate is also the                    value that the function increases by when <m>x</m> increases by 1 if the function is a growth function.  The growth rate is also the value that the function decreases by when <m>x</m> increases by 1 if the function is a decay function.</p>               
  </answer>
</task>     
<task>
  <statement>
    <p> How do you know this is a growth function? </p>
  </statement>
  <answer>
    <p> The function is a growth function because the growth factor is greater than 1.  If the growth factor was less than 1, then the function would be a decay function.</p>
  </answer>
</task> 
<task>
  <statement>
    <p>
      What is the domain and range of the function?
    </p>
  </statement>
  <answer>
    <p>
      The domain of the function is all real numbers.  The range of the function is all real numbers greater than 0.
    </p>
  </answer>   
</task>
</exercise>
<exercise>
 <introduction>
  <p> Look at the image below.  The graph is of the function <m>f(x)=2(0.5)^x</m>.  Use the graph to answer the following questions.  You may use Desmos to help you answer the questions.
  </p>

<image source="desmos-graph.2.0.5.x.svg" width="40%">
<shortdescription>decreasing exponential graph with y-intercept (0,2)</shortdescription>
</image>
</introduction>
<task>
  <statement>
    <p> What is the initial value of the function? </p>
  </statement>
  <answer>
    <p> The initial value is <m>2</m>.  This is the value of the function when <m>x=0</m>.  The initial value is also the <m>y</m>-intercept of the graph.  The initial value is also the value of <m>a</m> in the function <m>f(x)=ab^x</m>.</p>
  </answer>
</task>
<task>
  <statement>
    <p> What is the decay factor of the function? </p>
  </statement>    
  <answer>
    <p> The decay factor is <m>0.5</m>.  This is the value of <m>b</m> in the function <m>f(x)=ab^x</m>.  The decay factor is also the value that the function decreases by when <m>x</m> increases by 1.  The decay factor is also the value that the function increases by when <m>x</m> increases by 1 if the function is a growth function.</p>
  </answer>
</task>
<task>
  <statement>
    <p> What is the decay rate of the function? </p>
  </statement>
  <answer>  
    <p> The decay rate is <m>0.5</m> or <m>50%</m>.  The decay rate is the decay factor minus 1.  The decay rate is also the decay factor minus 1 expressed as a percent.  The decay rate is also the value that the function decreases by when <m>x</m> increases by 1 if the function is a decay function.  The decay rate is also the value that the function increases by when <m>x</m> increases by 1 if the function is a growth function.</p>      
  </answer>
</task>
<task>
  <statement> 
    <p> How do you know this is a decay function? </p>
  </statement>
  <answer>  
    <p> The function is a decay function because the decay factor is less than 1.  If the decay factor was greater than 1, then the function would be a growth function.</p>
  </answer>
</task>
<task>
  <statement>
    <p>
      What is the domain and range of the function?
    </p>
  </statement>        
  <answer>
    <p>
      The domain of the function is all real numbers.  The range of the function is all real numbers greater than 0.
    </p>
  </answer>
</task>
</exercise> 
<exercise>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Go to Desmos and type in the equations <m>y=ab^x</m>.  Click on "all" sliders.  Move the <m>a</m> slider, make observations, and answer the following questions.       
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>b > 1</m> and <m>a &lt; 0</m> What do you notice about the graph?  What is the domain and range of the function?  How does this graph differ from the graph when <m>a>0</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
  <answer>
    <p>
      The graph is a reflection of the graph when <m>a>0</m> across the <m>x</m>-axis.  The domain of the function is all real numbers.  The range of the function is all real numbers less than 0.
    </p>
  </answer>
</task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>0 &lt; b &lt; 1</m> and <m>a &lt; 0</m>.. What do you notice about the graph?  What is the domain and range of the function?  How does this graph differ from the graph when <m>a>0</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>  
      <p>
      The graph is a reflection of the graph when <m>a>0</m> across the <m>x</m>-axis.  The domain of the function is all real numbers.  The range of the function is all real numbers less than 0.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </task> 
</exercise>
<exercise>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Let <m>b=0</m> in the function <m>f(x)=ab^x</m>.  What do you notice about the graph? </p>
  </introduction>
<answer>
  
    <p>
      The graph is a horizontal line at <m>y=0</m>.  The domain of the function is all real numbers.  The range of the function is <m>{0}</m>.  
    </p>
  
</answer>
</exercise>
<exercise>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Let <m>b=1</m> in the function <m>f(x)=ab^x</m>.  What do you notice about the graph? </p>
  </introduction>
  <answer>
  
    <p>
      The graph is a horizontal line at <m>y=a</m>.  
    </p>  
  
  </answer>
</exercise>
<exercise>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Let <m>b \lt 0</m> in the function <m>f(x)=ab^x</m>.  What do you notice about the graph? </p>
  </introduction>
  <answer>
  
    <p>
      The graph is not a function.  The graph does not pass the vertical line test.
    </p>
   
</answer>
</exercise>
